Given what you pointed out about T/Q vertical structure being low-rank
(especially aloft), I'm going to port this using per-variable EOF/PCA
compression feeding a plain MLP, not a literal port of the 1D-CNN
encoder. Two reasons: it's the better-motivated design given your
point, and MATLAB's custom-training-loop API for 1D
convolution/pooling has enough fiddly dimension/format handling that I
can't verify it runs correctly without a MATLAB instance to test
against — I'd rather hand you something reliable than something that
looks plausible but breaks on a dimension mismatch. If you
specifically want the CNN version ported too, say so and I'll write it
with that caveat spelled out.

I don't have MATLAB available in this environment to actually run it, so treat this as a first draft to debug against, not verified-working code — please run it through and flag anything that errors.

A few notes on the translation:

Requires Deep Learning Toolbox (for dlarray, fullyconnect, adamupdate,
dlfeval, dlgradient) and Statistics and Machine Learning Toolbox (for
pca). Check you have both licensed before running.

pca() mapping from Python's sklearn.PCA: MATLAB's pca returns coeff
(loadings), score (projected coefficients), and mu (the mean it
subtracted) — this is a direct equivalent to sklearn's components_,
transform() output, and mean_. I used explained (percent variance per
component) to give you the cumulative-variance printout at the top,
which answers the "how many EOFs do I actually need" question from
earlier — check those printed numbers before trusting nPCA_T/Q/O3.

Training loop is manual (dlfeval/dlgradient/adamupdate in an explicit
loop over minibatches), rather than using trainNetwork or trainnet —
this mirrors the PyTorch training loop from the Python version fairly
directly and gives you full visibility into what's happening each
step, which is probably easier to debug coming from Matlab than a
black-box trainNetwork call would be.

fullyconnect(X, W, b) expects X formatted 'CB' (channels × batch) —
that's why you'll see ' transposes when building Xb/Yb each batch;
MATLAB's dlarray convention here is the reverse of the row-major
convention you'd default to.

The decoder is fixed, not trained — decoderW/decoderB are set from the
PCA fit once and never touched by adamupdate (they're simply not part
of the params struct passed to it), same as finetune_decoder=False in
the Python version.

Take your time with this one — it's denser than the Python version in
the linear-algebra bookkeeping (dlarray formats especially), which is
exactly the kind of thing that's easier to reason about slowly than to
have thrown at you all at once.

